-- js-attrs.rofl — AN ATTRIBUTE THE SCANNER EMITS AND NO RULE READS.
--
-- w_unconsumed_attribute, and its note is a list of four times a design note
-- claimed a fact was missing that was already on the store: `ast_attr(M, kind,
-- "get")` was the fourth, and the same key was consumed for `"constructor"` and
-- for nothing else. THE SHAPE IS ALWAYS THE SAME — the note was written while
-- looking at the RULES, and the store is a different artifact produced by a
-- scanner nobody is reading at that moment.
--
-- SO ASK THE RULES. This is the third audit built on the kernel's own
-- reification (see rules/js-vocabulary.rofl, whose `lit_rel` and `lit_arg` this
-- pack reads): clauses are stored as facts after parsing, so `which attribute
-- keys does any rule name` is a QUERY over `premise_lit` and not a grep.
--
-- IT NEEDS A CORPUS AND rules/js-vocabulary.rofl, which is why it is a pack of
-- its own: the vocabulary audit is deliberately corpus-free and costs 8.9% of
-- every world it is loaded into. This one has `ast_attr` on the left of every
-- rule and cannot be.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT A RULE NAMES, ARGUMENT BY ARGUMENT AND LITERAL BY LITERAL. `slot_atom`
-- in the vocabulary pack answers per POSITION, which is the wrong grain here:
-- the question is whether one literal names a key AND a value together, and a
-- key read with a variable value is a rule reading every value there is.
attr_lit(L, K, V)      :- lit_rel(L, ast_attr), lit_arg(L, 2, K), lit_arg(L, 3, V).
attr_lit_kvar(L, $var(X)) :- attr_lit(L, $var(X), _).
attr_lit_vvar(L, $var(X)) :- attr_lit(L, _, $var(X)).

-- a key some rule names by name
attr_key_read(K)       :- attr_lit(L, K, _), not attr_lit_kvar(L, K).
-- ...and a key some rule reads with the VALUE left free, which is a rule that
-- reads every value the scanner can put there. `unconsumed_value` must not fire
-- for those, or it would report every name in the corpus.
attr_key_read_free(K)  :- attr_lit(L, K, V), not attr_lit_kvar(L, K), attr_lit_vvar(L, V).
attr_pair_read(K, V)   :- attr_lit(L, K, V), not attr_lit_kvar(L, K), not attr_lit_vvar(L, V).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE TWO AUDITS, and they are two because the defect has two shapes. The first
-- is a whole key nobody reads — `async`, `generator`, `prefix`. The second is
-- the one the item was named for: a key that IS read, only ever with constant
-- values, and a value among them that no rule names.
-- TWO LISTS, AND THE SPLIT IS THE `not_a_construct` / `frame_deferred` SPLIT
-- rules/js-model.rofl already makes for kinds, for the same reason: a key the
-- model will never read is a different fact from one nobody has read YET, and
-- collapsing them is how a queue becomes a shrug.
--
-- THE CRITERION FOR THE FIRST LIST IS MEASURABLE, not a preference: the
-- distinction the attribute carries is ALSO carried by something the model
-- already reads — usually the node KIND. `optional` is `optional_member_expression`;
-- `method` is `object_method`; `tail` is the child index of the last quasi.
-- Reading the attribute would be a second spelling of a fact the model has.
edb(attr_unread_ok).       -- attr_unread_ok(Key, Reason)
edb(attr_deferred).        -- attr_deferred(Key, WorkItem)
edb(attr_value_unread_ok). -- attr_value_unread_ok(Key, Value, Reason)

unconsumed_attr[audit](K) :- ast_attr[code](_, K, _), not attr_key_read(K),
                             not attr_unread_ok(K, _), not attr_deferred(K, _).
unconsumed_value[audit](K, V) :- ast_attr[code](_, K, V), attr_key_read(K),
                                 not attr_key_read_free(K), not attr_pair_read(K, V),
                                 not attr_value_unread_ok(K, V, _).

-- ...AND BOTH EXCUSE LISTS ARE GUARDED THEMSELVES, on the precedent
-- `not_a_construct_unseen` set in rules/js-model.rofl: a misspelling in either
-- list silently readmits the key to nothing, and this audit is the only thing
-- that would have said so.
attr_unread_ok_unseen[audit](K)   :- attr_unread_ok(K, _), not ast_attr[code](_, K, _).
attr_unread_ok_read[audit](K)     :- attr_unread_ok(K, _), attr_key_read(K).
attr_value_ok_unseen[audit](K, V) :- attr_value_unread_ok(K, V, _), not ast_attr[code](_, K, V).
attr_deferred_unseen[audit](K)    :- attr_deferred(K, _), not ast_attr[code](_, K, _).
attr_deferred_read[audit](K)      :- attr_deferred(K, _), attr_key_read(K).
attr_double_excused[audit](K)     :- attr_unread_ok(K, _), attr_deferred(K, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A KEY THE RULES READ THROUGH A TABLE, WHICH THE TWO AUDITS ABOVE CANNOT SEE.
--
-- `attr_key_read` is a rule NAMING a key, and that is the whole of what it can
-- see. `rules/js-env.rofl` names none: it reads
-- `attr_needs(L, K, Key, V, F), ast_attr[code](N, Key, V)` — the key and the
-- value both arrive from a TABLE at evaluation time, so the literal carries two
-- variables and `attr_lit_kvar` correctly refuses to call it a key read. The
-- consequence was a wrong row rather than a missing one: `async` sat in
-- `attr_deferred` under the note *nobody has read it yet* while
-- `outside_attr_needs(js, await_expression, async, true, top_level_await)` read
-- it 398 times over this corpus. THE FIFTH INSTANCE OF THE SHAPE THIS ITEM IS
-- NAMED FOR, found by the item's own audit being blind to it.
--
-- SO DERIVE THE TABLE TOO. A rule that joins a two-variable `ast_attr` literal
-- to some OTHER relation carrying the same two variables is reading whatever
-- pairs that relation holds. That much is a query; going from the relation's
-- NAME to its ROWS is not, because Datalog cannot index a relation by name — so
-- the bridge is two lines by hand and `attr_table_unbridged[audit]` is what
-- makes the hand list impossible to forget. The next table gets a red gate, not
-- a silent hole.
--
-- BOTH LITERALS MUST BE POSITIVE, and that is not decoration: `unconsumed_value`
-- just above has exactly this shape — `ast_attr[code](_, K, V)` joined to
-- `attr_pair_read(K, V)` — with the second literal NEGATED. A negated co-literal
-- FILTERS the pairs; a positive one SUPPLIES them. Without the distinction this
-- audit reports itself.
pos_prem(R, L)    :- premise_lit(R, _, L), L = $lit(_, _, _, _).
attr_lit_kvvar(L) :- attr_lit(L, K, V), attr_lit_kvar(L, K), attr_lit_vvar(L, V).
attr_table_read(Rel, I, J) :- pos_prem(R, L), attr_lit_kvvar(L), attr_lit(L, K, V),
                              pos_prem(R, L2), lit_rel(L2, Rel), Rel != ast_attr,
                              lit_arg(L2, I, K), lit_arg(L2, J, V).

edb(attr_table_bridged).   -- attr_table_bridged(Relation)
attr_table_unbridged[audit](Rel, I, J) :- attr_table_read(Rel, I, J),
                                          not attr_table_bridged(Rel).
attr_table_bridged_unread[audit](Rel)  :- attr_table_bridged(Rel), not attr_table_read(Rel, _, _).

-- THE BRIDGE ITSELF, and it feeds both audits: a key read only through a table
-- is a key read, and the pairs it holds are pairs read.
attr_pair_read(K, V) :- attr_needs(_, _, K, V, _).
attr_pair_read(K, V) :- outside_attr_needs(_, _, K, V, _).
attr_key_read(K)     :- attr_pair_read(K, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHICH KINDS IN THIS POSITION CARRY NO SUCH ATTRIBUTE AT ALL.
--
-- `f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute`
-- (2026-09-09) ends with a question and the observation that nobody runs it:
-- *an attribute test on a heterogeneous parent is a claim that every parent HAS
-- the attribute, and the scanner's contract — every scalar own property, and
-- only those — makes that claim false wherever the grammar omits a field. Ask
-- of any `ast_attr(N, K, V)` guard: which kinds in this position carry no K at
-- all?* This is that query, derived from the rules rather than asked per guard.
--
-- THE POSITION IS THE GUARD'S OWN, taken from the clause: a constant-valued
-- `ast_attr($var(S), K, _)` premise whose subject variable S also appears as the
-- PARENT of an `ast_child($var(S), Field, _, _)` premise in the same rule. Four
-- such pairs exist over all eleven JS rule packs, and the field is what makes
-- the population meaningful — a bare sweep over every field reports 125 rows of
-- `computed`-is-absent-from-`block_statement`, which is true and useless.
--
-- THE MIRROR READING IS A DECLARED HOLE WITH NO SITE. A guard whose subject is
-- the CHILD of an `ast_child(_, Field, _, $var(S))` premise is a guard over a
-- position too, and this rule does not see it. Measured 2026-09-09: exactly two
-- clauses in the tree have that shape, both `kind`/`body` in the two
-- `class_method` arms of rules/js-callgraph.rofl, and both pin S to
-- `class_method` with an `ast_node` premise of their own — so the kind arm below
-- already covers them and the mirror reading would add nothing today. It is a
-- survivor waiting on a corpus, not on an argument.
--
-- AND THE POPULATION NARROWS WHERE THE RULE NARROWS IT. If the same clause also
-- pins S to a DECLARED kind through `ast_node($var(S), Kind, _, _)`, only that
-- kind is in the population — which is `delegates[flow]` pinning Y to
-- `yield_expression`, and it removes seven of the twelve rows. The kind test is
-- `node_kind(js, Kind)`, self-configuring on the precedent `kind_slot` sets in
-- rules/js-vocabulary.rofl, so a hand list of kind positions is not needed here
-- either. WHERE THE POPULATION COMES FROM A DERIVED RELATION OR A TABLE — as
-- `selects[flow]` and `field_init[code]` both do — this rule cannot narrow it,
-- and the residue is excused BY NAME below rather than hidden.
rule_prem(R, L)            :- premise_lit(R, _, L), L = $lit(_, _, _, _).
rule_prem(R, L)            :- premise_lit(R, _, $not(L)).
attr_test_lit(L, S, K)     :- attr_lit(L, K, V), lit_arg(L, 1, S),
                              not attr_lit_kvar(L, K), not attr_lit_vvar(L, V).
attr_test_svar(L, $var(X)) :- attr_test_lit(L, $var(X), _).
child_lit(L, S, F)         :- body_lit(L), lit_rel(L, ast_child),
                              lit_arg(L, 1, S), lit_arg(L, 2, F).
child_lit_svar(L, $var(X)) :- child_lit(L, $var(X), _).
child_lit_fvar(L, $var(X)) :- child_lit(L, _, $var(X)).

attr_guard_slot(R, K, S, F)    :- rule_prem(R, L), attr_test_lit(L, S, K), attr_test_svar(L, S),
                                  rule_prem(R, L2), child_lit(L2, S, F), child_lit_svar(L2, S),
                                  not child_lit_fvar(L2, F).
attr_guard_kind(R, K, S, Kind) :- rule_prem(R, L), attr_test_lit(L, S, K), attr_test_svar(L, S),
                                  rule_prem(R, L2), lit_rel(L2, ast_node), lit_arg(L2, 1, S),
                                  lit_arg(L2, 2, Kind), node_kind(js, Kind).
attr_guard_pinned(R, K, S)     :- attr_guard_kind(R, K, S, _).

attr_slot_gap(K, F, Kind) :- attr_guard_slot(R, K, S, F), not attr_guard_pinned(R, K, S),
                             ast_child[code](P, F, _, _), ast_node[code](P, Kind, _, _),
                             not ast_attr[code](P, K, _).
attr_slot_gap(K, F, Kind) :- attr_guard_slot(R, K, S, F), attr_guard_kind(R, K, S, Kind),
                             ast_child[code](P, F, _, _), ast_node[code](P, Kind, _, _),
                             not ast_attr[code](P, K, _).

-- ...AND THE POLARITY IS THE WHOLE GATE. A gap under a NEGATED test is harmless:
-- `not ast_attr(P, computed, true)` keeps a node with no `computed` at all, which
-- is what `key_name` in rules/js-structure.rofl relies on and says so in its own
-- comment. The SAME gap under a POSITIVE test silently drops those nodes. So the
-- report above carries both and the audit carries only the positive ones — and
-- swapping `key_name`'s negation for `ast_attr(P, computed, false)` moves two
-- rows from one to the other. That swap is the mutant
-- `f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute`
-- recorded as a SURVIVOR with no oracle, on the grounds that it moves no row in
-- any relation the model derives today. It moves a row here.
attr_guard_slot_pos(R, K, S, F) :- pos_prem(R, L), attr_test_lit(L, S, K), attr_test_svar(L, S),
                                   rule_prem(R, L2), child_lit(L2, S, F), child_lit_svar(L2, S),
                                   not child_lit_fvar(L2, F).
attr_pos_slot_gap(K, F, Kind) :- attr_guard_slot_pos(R, K, S, F), not attr_guard_pinned(R, K, S),
                                 ast_child[code](P, F, _, _), ast_node[code](P, Kind, _, _),
                                 not ast_attr[code](P, K, _).
attr_pos_slot_gap(K, F, Kind) :- attr_guard_slot_pos(R, K, S, F), attr_guard_kind(R, K, S, Kind),
                                 ast_child[code](P, F, _, _), ast_node[code](P, Kind, _, _),
                                 not ast_attr[code](P, K, _).

edb(attr_slot_gap_ok).    -- attr_slot_gap_ok(Key, Field, Kind, Reason)
attr_blind_guard[audit](K, F, Kind)   :- attr_pos_slot_gap(K, F, Kind),
                                         not attr_slot_gap_ok(K, F, Kind, _).
-- the excuse list is checked against the WHOLE report, not against the positive
-- half: an excuse that stops being needed because a guard was rewritten as a
-- negation is still a true row about the corpus, and reddening for that would
-- punish the repair.
attr_slot_gap_ok_unseen[audit](K, F, Kind) :- attr_slot_gap_ok(K, F, Kind, _),
                                              not attr_slot_gap(K, F, Kind).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ...AND THE HALF OF IT NO KIND-LEVEL READING CAN STATE. The finding above
-- explains the seven blind key positions as *babel gives a private name no such
-- field*, which is an assertion about a KIND, and a kind is the wrong unit:
-- measured here, `class_private_method` carries `computed` on ONE of its three
-- nodes and not on the other two. `get #face` has it and `#rim` and `#tucked` do
-- not, because babel's builder emits `computed` for the ACCESSOR forms of a
-- private method and not for the plain one. So the presence of an attribute can
-- depend on the value of ANOTHER attribute on the same node, and no reading of
-- the grammar by kind would say so.
--
-- THIS IS THE ONE THE SLOT AUDIT CANNOT SEE, in both directions: a kind that is
-- split is present in the slot, so `attr_slot_gap` finds no gap for it, and the
-- guard is blind to exactly the nodes that lack it. It is the audit with the
-- smallest answer here and the sharpest one.
attr_kind_has(Kind, K)   :- ast_node[code](P, Kind, _, _), ast_attr[code](P, K, _).
attr_kind_lacks(Kind, K) :- attr_kind_has(Kind, K), ast_node[code](P, Kind, _, _),
                            not ast_attr[code](P, K, _).

edb(attr_split_ok).       -- attr_split_ok(Key, Kind, Reason)
attr_kind_split[audit](K, Kind) :- attr_key_read(K), attr_kind_lacks(Kind, K),
                                   not attr_split_ok(K, Kind, _).
attr_split_ok_unseen[audit](K, Kind) :- attr_split_ok(K, Kind, _), not attr_kind_lacks(Kind, K).
